  "Number": 30,
  "Question": "If \\(xy=b\\) and \\(\\dfrac1{x^2}+\\dfrac1{y^2}=a,\\) then \\((x+y)^2\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\((a+2b)^2\\)",
    "B": "\\(a^2+b^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(b(ab+2)\\)",
    "D": "\\(ab(b+2)\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac1a+2b\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Expanding \\((x+y)^2\\) will require \\(x^2+y^2\\) and \\(xy\\). The second condition supplies the sum of squares after combining its fractions.\n\nThe given reciprocals require \\(x\\ne0\\) and \\(y\\ne0\\), so \\(b=xy\\ne0\\). Therefore\n\\[\na=\\frac1{x^2}+\\frac1{y^2}\n=\\frac{x^2+y^2}{x^2y^2}\n=\\frac{x^2+y^2}{b^2}.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(b^2\\) gives \\(x^2+y^2=ab^2\\). Now\n\\[\n(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2+2xy\n=ab^2+2b=b(ab+2).\n\\]\nThe result is \\(\\boxed{b(ab+2)}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
